[38;5;231m⏺[39m Three ways to shape the module. The fork is the stored layout and whether the module notifies consumers of changes —
  that decides the on-disk format (hard to change once users have data) and the call-site API (easy to change).

  - [1mA. Single JSON blob + declared defaults.[0m One key, [38;5;153mwebapp:prefs[39m, holding [38;5;153m{theme, rememberedUsername}[39m. The module owns
    a defaults table; reads merge stored values over defaults; unknown keys are dropped; unparseable JSON falls back to
    defaults rather than throwing. Buys: one place to reset, one place to version if the shape ever changes, and
    consumers that can't crash on bad data. Costs: a write to one preference rewrites the whole blob — irrelevant at
    this size.
  - [1mB. One key per preference.[0m [38;5;153mwebapp:theme[39m, [38;5;153mwebapp:rememberedUsername[39m. Buys: dead simple, no parsing. Costs: "reset
    all" and any future shape migration have to know every key that ever existed, and keys leak across the codebase.
    Rules out cheap versioning.
  - [1mC. A + change subscriptions.[0m Same store, plus [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m so the UI re-renders on change. Buys: reactivity. Costs:
    machinery for two settings on a page with no dynamic re-render — YAGNI today, and trivially added to A later.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's the only one of the three whose stored format you won't regret, and the subscription layer in C is
  additive if it's ever needed.
